WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.360
Two cellular floor plans.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:12.960
A prokaryote a bacterium is an open plan: few or no internal membranes, no true

3
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.820
nucleus, everything happening in one room.

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.880
A eukaryote your cells is a building of walled offices: a membrane-bound nucleus,

5
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.040
mitochondria, ER, Golgi, lysosomes, each doing one job.

6
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.340
The banner says it: compartments enable specialization.

7
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.080
That single architectural difference is the reason your cells can do things bacteria

8
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.080
cannot.

9
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:46.333
The plasma membrane is the cell's outer wall, and it's a phospholipid bilayer.

10
00:00:47.273 --> 00:00:52.793
Hydrophilic heads face the water on both sides; the hydrophobic core in the middle

11
00:00:52.793 --> 00:00:55.133
blocks most molecules from crossing.

12
00:00:56.153 --> 00:01:01.633
That makes the membrane a selective barrier impermeable to most solutes on its own,

13
00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:05.033
but studded with proteins that choose what passes.

14
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:11.653
Channel proteins open passages for specific ions; receptor proteins receive signals

15
00:01:11.653 --> 00:01:16.413
from outside. The membrane decides what gets in and what stays out.

16
00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:22.583
Two ways the cell moves cargo across that barrier, and a way we proved it.

17
00:01:23.163 --> 00:01:29.443
On the left, a clinical correlate: in familial hypercholesterolemia, broken LDL-receptor

18
00:01:29.443 --> 00:01:35.663
endocytosis leaves cholesterol piling up in the arteries endocytosis brings

19
00:01:35.663 --> 00:01:42.263
material in. On the right, the experiment that mapped the path out: George Palade's

20
00:01:42.263 --> 00:01:48.663
pulse-chase. Tag fresh protein with a brief radioactive pulse, chase with unlabeled,

21
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:54.723
and watch the labeled cohort travel from rough ER to Golgi to secretion in snapshots

22
00:01:54.723 --> 00:01:58.823
over time. That's how we know the secretory pathway is real.

23
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:03.457
Here's the chapter's whole argument in one disease.

24
00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:10.257
The lysosome is the cell's recycling center, full of digestive enzymes sealed safely

25
00:02:10.257 --> 00:02:11.257
behind a membrane.

26
00:02:12.177 --> 00:02:17.477
In Tay-Sachs, one of those enzymes — hexosaminidase A is missing.

27
00:02:18.477 --> 00:02:24.717
Without it, a fatty molecule called GM2 ganglioside can't be broken down, so it

28
00:02:24.717 --> 00:02:30.577
accumulates, the lysosome swells, and the cell, especially nerve cells, chokes:

29
00:02:31.477 --> 00:02:36.377
muscle weakness, neurological decline, death usually before age five.

30
00:02:37.477 --> 00:02:41.497
One enzyme, one compartment, one child's life.

31
00:02:42.797 --> 00:02:47.037
When a compartment fails, everything it was holding back comes apart.
